Status: Proposed

Valkey Kubernetes Operator RFC

Abstract

This RFC proposes the development of an open-source Kubernetes Operator for Valkey. 
The Operator will automate the provisioning, configuration, and lifecycle management of Valkey clusters within Kubernetes environments. 
It will support standalone and Sentinel-based deployments, integrate with cert-manager for TLS, expose Prometheus metrics, and support persistent storage configurations, offering production-grade capabilities for cloud-native environments.

Motivation

Operating Valkey at scale in Kubernetes environments remains complex and time-consuming. 
Most enterprise users currently manage Valkey deployments manually or via generic Helm charts, lacking production-readiness, automation, or compliance guarantees.

This Operator addresses those challenges by:
- Providing a Kubernetes-native way to deploy and manage Valkey
- Enabling self-service for developers via CRDs
- Automating TLS setup, monitoring, and failover
- Supporting advanced production use cases like Sentinel HA and persistent storage
- Matching enterprise expectations for security, observability, and compliance

Unlike Redis, which has various third-party operators with unclear support models, Valkey has the opportunity to lead with an officially supported Kubernetes-native experience.

Design considerations

The operator will be implemented using Kubebuilder and based on the Bitnami Helm chart for Valkey. 
It will expose a Custom Resource Definition (CRD) named `Valkey`, allowing users to declaratively deploy Valkey instances via YAML.

Key design principles:
- User-first: Apply 10 lines of YAML and get a working, HA Valkey deployment.
- Composability: Work alongside tools like cert-manager, Prometheus Operator, and Everest.
- Security: TLS and credential management must be default-safe.
- Modularity: Start simple (standalone + Sentinel), then iterate (autoscaling, dashboards).
- Observability: First-class Prometheus support out-of-the-box.

Comparative notes:
- SAP’s internal `valkey-operator` is optimized for Sentinel and metrics but is not distribution-friendly.
- Hyperspike’s operator is more lightweight and public, but lacks Sentinel and TLS support.
This RFC proposes an open-source Valkey operator that balances ease of use and production-grade features.

Specification

CRD Schema Overview

```yaml
apiVersion: cache.percona.com/v1alpha1
kind: Valkey
metadata:
  name: my-valkey
spec:
  replicas: 3
  sentinel:
    enabled: true
  metrics:
    enabled: true
  tls:
    enabled: true
  persistence:
    enabled: true
    type: both
    storageClass: fast-disks
    size: 5Gi
  config:
    configMapName: custom-valkey-conf
  modules:
    - name: redisbloom.so
      path: /opt/valkey/modules/redisbloom.so
```

Operator Behavior

- Deploys Bitnami Valkey chart under the hood.
- Supports 2 modes:
  - Standalone: 1 primary, optional replicas.
  - Sentinel: quorum-based HA with automatic failover.
- Exposes Valkey connection info as a Kubernetes `Secret`.
- Handles TLS setup via cert-manager or self-signed certs.
- Optionally loads custom config via ConfigMap.
- Optional metrics sidecar for Prometheus scraping.

Configuration

| Feature             | Config Options                                                                 |
|---------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Deployment Mode     | `spec.sentinel.enabled: true/false`                                           |
| TLS                 | `spec.tls.enabled: true` with cert-manager integration                         |
| Persistence         | `spec.persistence.type: rdb/aof/both`, `storageClass`, `size`                 |
| Modules             | `spec.modules`: list of `.so` modules to include                               |
| Monitoring          | `spec.metrics.enabled: true`                                                   |
| Custom Config       | `spec.config.configMapName`                                                   |
| Pod Customization   | node selectors, tolerations, resource limits, securityContext, etc.           |

Secret Output

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: valkey-test-binding
type: Opaque
stringData:
  host: valkey-test.svc
  port: "6379"
  password: ********
  sentinelHost: valkey-test.svc
  sentinelPort: "26379"
  tlsEnabled: "true"
```

Authentication and Authorization

- No RBAC-specific changes are needed outside standard Kubernetes role management.
- Valkey credentials are stored in Kubernetes Secrets.
- TLS certs issued via cert-manager will follow existing RBAC rules.

RDB

- Persistence modes include:
  - `rdb`: periodic snapshotting
  - `aof`: append-only logging
  - `both`: hybrid approach (default for production)

Configuration (Runtime)

- Users can override default Helm values using CR fields or a `valkey.conf` file via ConfigMap.
- Sensitive data like passwords are not stored in the config map and are auto-generated and stored in Secrets.

Keyspace notifications

N/A – this Operator does not modify Valkey internals; only orchestrates deployment.

Cluster mode

- Sentinel mode uses quorum-based failover.
- Standalone mode supports scaling replicas.

Module API

- Users can opt-in to modules like `Search`, `Bloom`, and `JSON` by including them in the container image and enabling via `valkey.conf`.

Replication

- Sentinel handles failover logic.
- Operator ensures correct replica configurations based on `spec.replicas`.

Dependencies

- Bitnami Valkey Helm Chart
- cert-manager (optional)
- Prometheus Operator (optional)

Benchmarking

Target SLAs:
- P95 provisioning time: <60s
- 99.9% uptime for Sentinel deployments
- ≥80% usage via CRs (vs manual installs)
- <1 manual intervention per 100 deployments

Testing

Automated tests will include:
- CR validation
- Smoke tests on Minikube
- Sentinel failover simulation
- TLS Secret injection
- Custom ConfigMap override

CI/CD pipeline to be integrated with GitHub Actions.

Observability

- Prometheus metrics sidecar container included.
- Optional ServiceMonitor created if Prometheus Operator is installed.
- Success metric: 100% Prometheus scrape coverage when metrics enabled.

Debug mechanism

- Operator logs will include CR status transitions.
- Valkey pod logs exposed via standard `kubectl logs`.
- Failures surfaced via CR `.status` field with conditions.


Sentinel-Based HA Valkey Topology Support

This RFC also incorporates support for a production-grade, Sentinel-based HA deployment strategy for Valkey in Kubernetes. 
The proposed operator will reflect learnings from past experience managing Valkey via customized Helm charts and StatefulSets, optimizing for resilience, AZ-awareness, and automation.

Goals

- Shard-based deployments (per cache type) using Sentinel-managed HA
- Flexible deployment of masters, replicas, and Sentinel in separate StatefulSets
- AZ-aware scheduling to avoid cross-AZ traffic and latency
- Clear control over replica counts and placement
- Optional integration of a custom controller for master awareness and controlled failover

Design Overview

Each Valkey Shard will be backed by multiple Kubernetes resources:

- A StatefulSet for the master (1 pod, zone-pinned, highest priority)
- One or more StatefulSets for replicas, deployed in secondary/tertiary zones
- A StatefulSet for Sentinels, decoupled from Valkey for scaling flexibility
- Dedicated Services per pod for connection management and DNS discovery

Failover orchestration may be introduced via an optional `Failover` CRD and controller logic to promote a specific pod or trigger automatic failover.

Custom resource example (`ValkeyShard`):

```yaml
apiVersion: example.com/v1alpha
kind: ValkeyShard
metadata:
  name: cache-0
spec:
  replicas: 3
  image: valkey:7.2.5-alpine
  shardIndex: 0
  parentRedis: cache
  config:
    maxmemory: "3gb"
  sentinel:
    config:
      down-after-milliseconds: "10000"
      failover-timeout: "180000"
```

Custom resource example (`Failover`):

```yaml
apiVersion: example.com/v1alpha
kind: Failover
metadata:
  name: failover-cache-0
spec:
  redisShard: cache-0
  targetMaster: cache-0-server-2
  force: false
  timeout: 300s
```

Benefits

- Reduced disruption by decoupling sidecars (e.g., filebeat, sentinel)
- Predictable master placement across availability zones
- Flexible read-replica configuration (scale to 1 or 2 as needed)
- Clearer operational visibility via CR `.status` fields (e.g., current master)
- Future-proofed for Sentinel replacements

This topology is already under internal testing via a custom operator built during an internal hackathon, with promising results. This RFC proposes evolving that prototype into a supported, production-grade capability.


